116. Locking
Locks prevent multiple users from modifying the same data simultaneously.
Common Locking Challenges
1. Blocking
Blocking occurs naturally when one transaction holds a lock on a resource and a second transaction requests an incompatible lock on the same resource. 
The second transaction is forced to wait until the first commits or rolls back. Severe blocking can degrade application performance.
2. Deadlock
A deadlock happens when two transactions are blocked because each holds a lock that the other needs to proceed.
Example: Transaction A locks Row 1 and requests Row 2. Simultaneously, Transaction B locks Row 2 and requests Row 1.

117. Read vs Write Locks
Read locks allow multiple users to look at data at the same time
write locks  allow to a single user to modify data.

